# CSS Transitions

Most of this specialization is about JavaScript, and since we are on animation it is worth a couple of lessons on CSS, because CSS is where most animation belongs.

Everything we built in the last three lessons ran through the JavaScript engine on every single frame. **CSS transitions** hand the whole job to the browser: we say what should change and how long it should take, and the browser does the rest.

## The `transition` Property

A **CSS transition** smoothly renders a change from one CSS value to another. You declare it with the `transition` property, and it takes up to four parts:

```text
transition: <property> <duration> <timing-function> <delay>
```

- The **property** to animate, such as `color`, or the special value `all` for every property.
- The **duration**. Note that unlike almost everything in JavaScript, CSS durations are in **seconds**, so `0.5s`, not `500`.
- Optionally a **timing function**, which is CSS's word for an easing function.
- Optionally a **delay** before it starts.

Most of the time you write two or three of those.

```{html autorun}
<a href="#">Hover over this link</a>
```
```+css
a {
  color: blue;
  font-size: 1.4em;
  transition: color 0.5s;
}
a:hover {
  color: red;
}
```

Hover on and off. The color eases between blue and red in both directions, and there is no JavaScript on the page at all.

## Put the Transition on the Permanent Selector

Here is the mistake that is easy to make and confusing to debug. Compare these two cells.

```{html autorun}
<a href="#" class="wrong">Wrong: transition on the hover rule</a>
```
```+css
.wrong {
  color: blue;
  font-size: 1.4em;
}
.wrong:hover {
  color: red;
  transition: color 1s;
}
```

Hover on: it fades in nicely. Hover off: it snaps back instantly.

The reason is that `.wrong:hover` only applies **while** the pointer is over the element. The moment you leave, that rule stops applying, the transition declaration goes with it, and there is nothing left to animate the change back.

**Put the `transition` on the selector that always applies:**

```{html autorun}
<a href="#" class="right">Right: transition on the base rule</a>
```
```+css
.right {
  color: blue;
  font-size: 1.4em;
  transition: color 1s;
}
.right:hover {
  color: red;
}
```

Now it fades both ways, because `.right` applies the whole time.

## Timing Functions

CSS has several easing functions built in, using the vocabulary from the last lesson:

- **`ease`** starts quickly and eases out at the end. **This is the default** if you do not name one. It is a slightly odd curve, and the reason for it is that starting fast makes an interface feel snappy while easing out still gives a sense of inertia.
- **`linear`** is constant speed, no easing.
- **`ease-in`** starts slowly and ends abruptly.
- **`ease-out`** starts abruptly and ends slowly.
- **`ease-in-out`** is slow at both ends, the slow-in-slow-out principle.

Hover across all five and compare:

```{html autorun}
<div class="bar t-ease">ease (default)</div>
<div class="bar t-linear">linear</div>
<div class="bar t-in">ease-in</div>
<div class="bar t-out">ease-out</div>
<div class="bar t-inout">ease-in-out</div>
```
```+css
.bar {
  width: 120px;
  padding: 6px;
  margin: 4px 0;
  background-color: #00274c;
  color: white;
  font-size: 0.9em;
  white-space: nowrap;
}
.bar:hover { width: 340px; }
.t-ease   { transition: width 1.5s ease; }
.t-linear { transition: width 1.5s linear; }
.t-in     { transition: width 1.5s ease-in; }
.t-out    { transition: width 1.5s ease-out; }
.t-inout  { transition: width 1.5s ease-in-out; }
```

You can also define your own curve with `cubic-bezier(...)`. Writing those numbers by hand is unpleasant, so use a visual tool: [cubic-bezier.com](https://cubic-bezier.com/), built by Lea Verou, lets you drag the curve and copy out the CSS.

## Transitioning Several Properties

Separate them with commas:

```{html autorun}
<div class="multi">Hover me</div>
```
```+css
.multi {
  width: 120px;
  padding: 12px;
  background-color: white;
  color: #00274c;
  border: 2px solid #00274c;
  transition: background-color 0.6s ease-in-out,
              color 0.6s ease-in-out,
              width 1.2s ease-in-out;
}
.multi:hover {
  width: 260px;
  background-color: #00274c;
  color: #ffcb05;
}
```

Three properties, and the width takes twice as long as the colors, which you can watch happen.

## Not Everything Can Be Transitioned

Most CSS properties are **animatable**: anything with a smooth range of values in between, such as `color`, `opacity`, `width`, `left`, `transform`.

Some are not. There is no meaningful halfway point between one `background-image` and another, or between `visibility: hidden` and `visible`. Those properties change instantly, and a `transition` on them does nothing.

```{html autorun}
<div class="animatable">opacity: animatable</div>
<div class="not-animatable">visibility: not animatable</div>
```
```+css
.animatable, .not-animatable {
  width: 220px; padding: 8px; margin: 4px 0;
  background-color: #00274c; color: white;
}
.animatable { opacity: 1; transition: opacity 1s; }
.animatable:hover { opacity: 0.15; }
.not-animatable { transition: visibility 1s; }
.not-animatable:hover { visibility: hidden; }
```

The first fades. The second vanishes, immediately, however long a duration you give it. If you want something to fade out and then be gone, `opacity` is the property to animate.

## Exercises

1. Add a transition to `.box` so that `background-color` changes over `0.3s`, and make sure it animates in **both** directions.

    ```html id=page1
    <div class="box"></div>
    ```

    ```{css}
    .box {
      width: 120px;
      height: 60px;
      background-color: steelblue;
      /* Write your transition here */
    }

    .box:hover {
      background-color: orange;
    }
    ```
    ```+id=page1
    ```
    ```+solution
    .box {
      width: 120px;
      height: 60px;
      background-color: steelblue;
      transition: background-color 0.3s;
    }

    .box:hover {
      background-color: orange;
    }
    ```
    ```+test
    assertRule('.box').should('have.css', 'transition-duration: 0.3s').run('Add a `transition` to `.box` lasting `0.3s`', 'Added a transition');
    assertRule('.box').should('have.css', 'transition-property: background-color').run('The transition should name `background-color`', 'Transitioned the background color');
    wrap(/\.box:hover\s*\{[^}]*transition/.test(source)).should('equal', false).run('Keep the `transition` out of the `:hover` rule, or it will not animate on the way back', 'Put the transition on the permanent selector');
    ```

2. Transition **two** properties on `.card`: `opacity` over `0.4s` and `width` over `0.8s`.

    ```html id=page2
    <div class="card">Hover me</div>
    ```

    ```{css}
    .card {
      width: 100px;
      padding: 10px;
      background-color: #00274c;
      color: white;
      opacity: 0.5;
      /* Write your transition here */
    }

    .card:hover {
      width: 250px;
      opacity: 1;
    }
    ```
    ```+id=page2
    ```
    ```+solution
    .card {
      width: 100px;
      padding: 10px;
      background-color: #00274c;
      color: white;
      opacity: 0.5;
      transition: opacity 0.4s, width 0.8s;
    }

    .card:hover {
      width: 250px;
      opacity: 1;
    }
    ```
    ```+test
    assertRule('.card').should('have.css', 'transition-property: opacity, width').run('Transition both `opacity` and `width`, separated by a comma', 'Named both properties');
    assertRule('.card').should('have.css', 'transition-duration: 0.4s, 0.8s').run('`opacity` should take `0.4s` and `width` should take `0.8s`', 'Gave each its own duration');
    ```

3. A transition works when hovering onto an element but snaps back instantly when the pointer leaves. What is wrong?

    ```{mcq}
    Fading in works; fading out does not. What is the cause?
    correct: Correct! The `transition` declaration is inside the `:hover` rule, which stops applying the instant the pointer leaves, so there is nothing to animate the change back. It belongs on the rule that always applies.
    wrong: Revisit "Put the Transition on the Permanent Selector".
    [x] The `transition` was declared inside the `:hover` rule instead of the base rule
    [ ] The duration is too short to see on the way out
    [ ] `:hover` cannot be transitioned at all
    [ ] The timing function needs to be `ease-in-out`
    ```

4. Which timing function does CSS use if you do not name one?

    ```{mcq}
    `transition: color 0.5s;` names no timing function. Which one is used?
    correct: Correct! The default is `ease`, which starts quickly and eases out. It is not `linear`, which is a common assumption.
    wrong: Look back at the list of timing functions and which one is marked as the default.
    [x] `ease`
    [ ] `linear`
    [ ] `ease-in-out`
    [ ] None; the change happens instantly
    ```

